+## Command Code integration reference
+
+Command Code follows Codenotch's `CommandCodeCredentials.swift`, `CommandCodeProvider.swift`, `CommandCodeUsage.swift` and `CommandCodeProfile.swift` at the pinned reference commit. The credential is Command Code's own API key. An AgentSpeed-managed key saved from Settings wins; otherwise a nonempty `COMMAND_CODE_API_KEY`; otherwise the read-only `~/.commandcode/auth.json` field `apiKey`. A saved-key store that exists but is unreadable, unsafe or corrupt is a credential error and never silently falls back to the environment token or CLI file. Command Code owns login and key rotation. The same key authenticates the CLI and Provider API. Never spawn the CLI, scrape its interactive `/usage` text, extract browser cookies, write the credential file or request a model completion to read quota.
+
+Queries run on the source's owning host against the fixed origin `https://api.commandcode.ai`, with bearer authorization, `Accept: application/json`, a fifteen-second timeout and no redirects. The account response is `GET /alpha/whoami?limits=1`; use only its organization ID and safe user/organization labels. Then query `GET /alpha/billing/credits` and `GET /alpha/billing/subscriptions`, adding `orgId` only when nonempty. Optional monthly-credit totals come from `monthlyCreditsGranted`; known plan IDs may supply only the documented included-credit allocation. Never infer a percentage for an unknown plan or invent a monthly amount. The billing routes are the CLI's internal `/alpha` surface, not the documented Provider API or a stable public contract. No supported noninteractive quota command exists yet, so treat alpha schema changes as an adapter failure, not a reason to switch to cookie or terminal scraping.
+
+`credits.monthlyCredits` is included monthly credit remaining. Monthly used fraction is `(grant - remaining) / grant`, and the reset is the subscription's `currentPeriodEnd`. The billing period duration may be derived only from valid `currentPeriodStart` and `currentPeriodEnd`. `purchasedCredits + freeCredits` is a separate on-demand balance: it never receives a fabricated percentage or reset and never counts toward the rolling windows, because Command Code spends and limits it separately. `windowLimits.fiveHour` and `windowLimits.weekly` each contribute an individual window when `used` is numeric and `cap` is positive; `usedFraction = used / cap` is not clamped, `remaining = 1 - usedFraction`, duration is five hours or seven days, and `resetAt` is Unix milliseconds with zero/absent meaning unknown. Preserve known zero and over-limit readings. Include every actual window rather than selecting only the tightest.
+
+HTTP 401/403 is `needsAuth`; clear the previous observation and remember the rejected credential so an unchanged key cannot expose another account's values. A 404 is `unsupported`. HTTP 429 respects Retry-After and otherwise establishes at least a sixty-second persisted deadline. Transport, service and decoding failures preserve only credential-matching stale observations with their original timestamps; otherwise they are errors with cold/server backoff. A successful poll imposes no hard refresh floor. Automatic application polling remains five minutes, concurrent refreshes coalesce, and credentials, schedules and structured observations never enter logs, wire snapshots or raw supplier payloads. Command Code carries no verified nonsecret account claim for remote `accountIdentity`; keep it null. The status flags `belowThreshold` and `windowLimits.exceeded` are not authoritative for exhaustion; use the reported balances and window values.
+
+Command Code allows multiple isolated CLI homes in Codenotch, but this adapter exposes the currently selected default account only. Adding multiple bindable accounts requires an explicit source-identity and account-claim design; do not derive one from the API key or labels.
+
+### Command Code API key settings and remote provisioning
+
+Subscription Settings offers a masked Command Code API key editor for the local source and each enabled SSH host, alongside the Kimi Code editor. It works without a detected CLI account or an enabled quota source. Users enter a Command Code API key and save/verify or remove it; they never edit a remote file or run provisioning commands. The editor shows only stored-key presence, never the saved secret, and keeps typed text out of periodic page-state snapshots. Disconnected destinations cannot claim a successful save. Saving configuration does not authorize supplier requests when network consent is disabled; persistence, verification and supplier rejection are reported separately.
+
+An explicitly saved key is authoritative for its host, takes precedence over `COMMAND_CODE_API_KEY` and the CLI file, and has no CLI login lifetime. A rejected, corrupt or inaccessible saved-key store must not silently change accounts by falling back to the environment token or CLI file; report the credential error instead. Removing a saved key permits subsequent environment/CLI discovery without altering the CLI-owned file. Key replacement and deletion invalidate in-flight old-identity results, queued observations and old displayed observations, and the provider re-reads credentials before committing any result.
+
+Local keys use system-backed secret storage (current-user DPAPI on Windows and Keychain on macOS), never ordinary settings JSON, UserDefaults, observation caches or exports; metadata reads expose only configured state and never decrypt the key. Remote keys are stored atomically as the key plus a fresh random internal revision nonce in `~/.agent-speed/quota/commandcode.json`, an owned regular file with mode `0600` under owned private `0700` directories, with the same safety rules as the Kimi key file. Every set generates a new revision, including delete/recreate and re-saving an identical key. That revision participates in the cache and rejection identity so an explicit re-save clears persisted rejection and backoff, while simply reloading the unchanged saved key does not. The nonce is never returned in metadata.
+
+Remote management is a separate correlated control message, not a quota snapshot: `type: "commandcode_credentials"`, a 1–128-character printable ASCII `requestId` (bytes 33–126), and `operation: "get"`, `"set"` or `"delete"`. Only `set` includes `apiKey` (1–4096 printable non-whitespace ASCII bytes); there is no region field. Reject unexpected fields, duplicate members and invalid types. Commands stay within the existing 16-KiB frame bound and use the existing no-PTY SSH stdin channel, never command arguments or a shell command.
+
+Reply only to the requesting subscriber with `type: "commandcode_credentials_result"`, matching `requestId` and `status: "ok"` or `"error"`. Successful replies report exactly `configured` (whether an AgentSpeed key is saved); failures report a fixed `errorCode` (`"invalid_request"` or `"storage_error"`) without raw exception text. No response includes the key, fragments, a fingerprint, the internal revision or filesystem paths. A set/delete acknowledgment means durable storage/removal succeeded, not that supplier authentication succeeded; these operations never request supplier HTTP. Snapshot `configured` is true whenever the saved key, environment token or CLI file exists on the owning host, and false only when all three are absent, so removing the saved key does not make an otherwise configured host disappear. The key never enters quota snapshots, persisted observation state, reply bodies, diagnostics or logs.
+
 ## Reference and attribution
 
 Endpoint and response research consulted [Codenotch](https://github.com/vinzdg/codenotch), particularly `Sources/Providers/CodexLocalProvider.swift` and `Sources/Providers/CodexUsage.swift`. Codenotch is MIT licensed, copyright (c) 2026 Vinz. This specification and its synthetic examples describe observed facts and do not reproduce Codenotch source code. If source code or a substantial portion is copied into the implementation, retain its full MIT copyright and permission notice with that code.
